{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review of chess ITEM 3, the public-API surface for the external importer, at exact clean pushed head 2867e08818c68e9144bac947bb70f122f075c927 on request/chess-item3-public-api, now rebased onto current main 5a9529b3. Governing assignment a1871572; spec notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md item 3. The three blockers in your changes-requested report 5f422357, since ratified, are closed at this head. (1) Open bound off an unverified frontier: Open now keeps the Log its own Records call returned and passes that exact verified head to apphost.BindingInForce, which takes the revision to scan instead of always walking kernel.Ref(genesis); the ref is never consulted a second time, so no concurrent advance can select a binding from a frontier the returned workspace did not verify. internal/app keeps naming the ref, because its audit runs later when the fold first reads the log, and that is stated at the call site and on the page. An empty revision is refused rather than guessed. (2) Append wrote payload objects before validation: it now derives the tree identity with gitstore.HashPayloadTree, which reproduces WritePayloadTree's object identity without writing or invoking Git, and kernel.Submit performs the single bounded write after intent, size, field and signature checks; a refused act leaves no objects, and a valid act no longer writes the same tree twice. (3) Unbounded PayloadCeiling narrowing: I took your second branch and removed the signed narrowing rather than adding a ceiling rejection at Init, because the clamp also covers repositories this package did not create. apphost.readLimit clamps a ceiling above MaxInt64 to MaxInt64, which loses nothing since no blob Git can store reaches that size, so such a repository now opens instead of reading every binding record as unreadable. Please say if you would still prefer an explicit refusal at Init as well. Fresh artifacts at this head, each